# These are durable, verifiable facts about well-known figures. They
kundiman | definition | The kundiman is a love song of Filipino origin, and it is significant beyond being traditional because its structure is built for two voices: the first line is sung by one person, the second is the response, and the song is therefore a conversation rather than a statement. That form reflects its origin, since it was adapted from Spanish and Mexican forms that came to the Philippines through the colonial period, and the words are usually Tagalog while the melodic shape and harmonic accompaniment are largely from those older traditions. It is normally sung a cappella or with a small ensemble, since the vocal line carries the argument, and the use of a minor mode gives the genre its characteristic gravity. The genre has also become a stage show with modern arrangements, which is how many people now first encounter it, and that form keeps the call and response while adding orchestration. It is treated as part of the national cultural identity and is taught in schools, which is unusual for a folk genre and reflects how firmly it has been claimed as heritage. | love song, call and response, traditional, folk, a cappella, minor, heritage | what is kundiman, what is the structure of a kundiman, is kundiman a filipino love song | kundiman | kundiman
impressionism | definition | Impressionism is a movement in painting from the late nineteenth century, and the name comes from a hostile review that mocked the loose visible brushwork, which is why the movement's own label is slightly an insult. The painters, Monet and Renoir among them, were working outdoors and painting quickly to catch the effect of a particular light at a particular moment, which is the real subject of the work. That is a genuine break from earlier painting, which was organised around subject matter and finished composition rather than about a view, and it is also why an impressionist picture of the same scene painted twice is two different pictures rather than a worse and a better version of one. The brushstroke is left visible, the edges are not always finished, and the colour is often mixed on the canvas rather than in precise amounts, which was a practical consequence of speed. The later development of post-impressionism is the term for the painters, including Van Gogh and Cezanne, who took the same approach and pushed it somewhere else. | monet, renoir, brushwork, light, outdoor, post impressionism, movement | what is impressionism, what is the impressionist movement, who started impressionism | impressionism | impressionism

